# Proposal

## Problem statement

<!-- Start with a concise description of the problem you're trying to solve. Don't talk about possible solutions yet. -->

While `Duration::from_{weeks,days,hours,mins}` constructors and `Duration::{WEEK,DAY,HOUR,MINUTE}` associated constants have been implemented, there is no way to get the number of whole weeks, days, hours, and minutes contained by [`Duration`](https://doc.rust-lang.org/core/time/struct.Duration.html).

To get a value from `Duration` in these units, division is required:

```rust
let duration = Duration::from_hours(6);
assert_eq!(duration.as_secs() / 3600, 6);
```

In [`time::SignedDuration`](https://docs.rs/time/0.3.55/time/struct.SignedDuration.html), [`chrono::TimeDelta`](https://docs.rs/chrono/0.4.45/chrono/struct.TimeDelta.html), and [`jiff::SignedDuration`](https://docs.rs/jiff/0.2.38/jiff/struct.SignedDuration.html), if a constructor for a given unit is implemented, then an extractor for that same unit is also guaranteed to be implemented.

## Motivating examples or use cases

<!-- Next add any motivating examples. Examples should ideally be real world examples, or minimized versions of the real world example in scenarios where the motivating code is not open source. Don't propose changes you think might *hypothetically* be useful; real use cases help make sure we have the right design. -->

Getting a value of these units through division is widely used:

- `duration.as_secs() / 60`: <https://github.com/search?q=%22.as_secs%28%29+%2F+60%22+language%3ARust&type=code>
- `duration.as_secs() / 3600`: <https://github.com/search?q=%22.as_secs%28%29+%2F+3600%22+language%3ARust&type=code>
- `duration.as_secs() / 86400`: <https://github.com/search?q=%22.as_secs%28%29+%2F+86400%22+language%3ARust&type=code>

`duration.as_secs() / 604_800` and other methods for getting the number of whole weeks are not widely used.

## Solution sketch

<!--
Please write down all the functions, types or traits that you propose here. Make sure you include the *full type signatures* (arguments, return type, trait bounds etc.).

You don't have to include the function bodies, but the signatures are a critical portion of the API and it is really difficult to evaluate the proposal without them.
-->

`#![feature(duration_extractors)]`:

```rust
// core::time

impl Duration {
    /// Returns the number of _whole_ weeks contained by this `Duration`.
    ///
    /// The returned value does not include any fractional component
    /// corresponding to units less than a week of the duration.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// #![feature(duration_extractors)]
    /// #![feature(duration_constructors)]
    /// use std::time::Duration;
    ///
    /// let duration = Duration::from_days(40);
    /// assert_eq!(duration.as_weeks(), 5);
    /// ```
    #[unstable(feature = "duration_extractors", issue = "none")]
    #[must_use]
    #[inline]
    pub const fn as_weeks(&self) -> u64 {
        self.as_secs() / (SECS_PER_MINUTE * MINS_PER_HOUR * HOURS_PER_DAY * DAYS_PER_WEEK)
    }

    /// Returns the number of _whole_ days contained by this `Duration`.
    ///
    /// The returned value does not include any fractional component
    /// corresponding to units less than a day of the duration.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// #![feature(duration_extractors)]
    /// use std::time::Duration;
    ///
    /// let duration = Duration::from_hours(130);
    /// assert_eq!(duration.as_days(), 5);
    /// ```
    #[unstable(feature = "duration_extractors", issue = "none")]
    #[must_use]
    #[inline]
    pub const fn as_days(&self) -> u64 {
        self.as_secs() / (SECS_PER_MINUTE * MINS_PER_HOUR * HOURS_PER_DAY)
    }
}
```

