It looks like this has been improperly sealed since `SliceIndex` was first introduced.

```rust
#![feature(slice_get_slice)]
#![allow(unused_variables)]

pub struct Foo;

impl ::std::slice::SliceIndex<Foo> for u32 {
    type Output = i32;

    fn get(self, slice: &[Foo]) -> Option<&Self::Output> { panic!() }
    fn get_mut(self, slice: &mut [Foo]) -> Option<&mut Self::Output> { panic!() }
    unsafe fn get_unchecked(self, slice: &[Foo]) -> &Self::Output { panic!() }
    unsafe fn get_unchecked_mut(self, slice: &mut [Foo]) -> &mut Self::Output { panic!() }
    fn index(self, slice: &[Foo]) -> &Self::Output { panic!() }
    fn index_mut(self, slice: &mut [Foo]) -> &mut Self::Output { panic!() }
}
```

[compiles on Rust 1.15](https://godbolt.org/z/1Mrcb7P9q) (released 2017-02-02). Rust 1.14 says the `std::slice::SliceIndex` isn't found.

@Thomas-Mewily congrats on relying on a nearly 10 year old mistake 😆

100% agree. I'm sure Predrag will figure something out with c-s-c if nothing else. I swear he lives for stuff like this.

Removing the `impl` restriction label as it is now confirmed to not be the cause.

@rustbot label -F-impl-restriction

